    "recent_exchanges": [
      {
        "user": "I turn 65 in November, have no health insurance now, and want to understand my Medicare choices. I am new to this.",
        "assistant": "Since you're new to this, the big picture is: Medicare starts with Part A for hospital-type care and Part B for doctors, outpatient care, tests, equipment, and many preventive services. Then there are two main ways to get that coverage: Original Medicare, where Parts A and B stay with the federal program and you can add drug coverage and possibly Medigap, or Medicare Advantage, which is a private plan that provides Parts A and B and often includes drug coverage.",
        "question": "When would you like Medicare coverage to start?"
      },
      {
        "user": "I do not know when I want coverage to start; I need help understanding the choices first. Are Original Medicare and Medicare Advantage two policies I buy together?",
        "assistant": "No—these are usually two different ways to receive your Medicare coverage, not two policies you buy together. Original Medicare means Part A and Part B stay with the federal program; Medicare Advantage is a private plan that provides Part A and Part B instead. With Original Medicare, you can add separate drug coverage and possibly Medigap, while Medigap does not go with Medicare Advantage.",
        "question": "What matters most when you compare these two paths—doctors, prescriptions, total cost, predictable bills, travel, or something else?"
      }
    ],
    "current_message": "I travel and want to keep my doctor. What difference does that make between the two paths? I do not know what my doctor accepts.",
